{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BJe8tSUDUbSvNtVbbwu5eF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full PDF paper and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch the full review process with 2 reviewers as specified:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nI'll launch both reviewers in parallel as independent agents, then conduct the roundtable synthesis.\n\nLet me carefully analyze what I have:\n\n**Paper**: \"Solver Trace Ablation: Dense Structural Rewards for Formal Reasoning via Mutation Testing\"\n- Proposes STA, adapting mutation testing's RIP framework to derive dense structural rewards for RL training of formal reasoning models\n\n\n- Tests across FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, and SMT-LIB/Z3 with ISR (Intensional Structure Ratio) as the key metric for measuring structural necessity\n- Claims STA-rewarded GRPO maintains prove rates while preserving structural integrity across 10 pages with 12 tables and 6 references\n\nNow I'm checking the review context against what's actually covered in the paper. The 12 planned experiments include FOL baselines, STA variants, and held-out evaluations, and I'm cross-referencing which tables correspond to each experiment to understand the experimental coverage.\n\nAll 12 experiments are accounted for across the tables, so the paper has solid experimental breadth. Now I'm ready to launch the parallel reviews.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39311,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39311},"output_tokens":15,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db36cc0b-3691-41e1-9901-01ea32ffa94b","uuid":"d7442adc-a3a8-4aa0-83db-82bd365c5f19"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BJe8tSUDUbSvNtVbbwu5eF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paper and review context loaded. Launching two parallel reviewers: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39311,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39311},"output_tokens":15,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db36cc0b-3691-41e1-9901-01ea32ffa94b","uuid":"b08dd63f-cbcf-4eef-9a7c-781a1f568ebe"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BJe8tSUDUbSvNtVbbwu5eF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JsSqVCrsrp5pzzVYEXXtj6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Solver Trace Ablation: Dense Structural Rewards for Formal Reasoning via Mutation Testing\"\n\nThe paper proposes Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a reward design methodology grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing. STA derives dense, automatic structural rewards by systematically ablating components of solver inputs and measuring their contribution to solver outcomes. The core metric is the Intensional Structure Ratio (ISR) = (1/n) Σ 𝟙[S(f_{-i}) ≠ S(f)], measuring the fraction of structurally necessary components.\n\nThe combined reward is r(f) = α·r_exec(f) + β·r_exec(f)·ISR(f), with a sigmoid curriculum for β.\n\nA 5-step derivation protocol (pilot → collect shortcuts → classify → map RIP → design checks) instantiates STA for any solver-language pair.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Setup**: Qwen3-4B (primary), Qwen3-8B (scaling). GRPO with K=8, 500 steps. FOLIO dataset for FOL; equivalent Prolog/SMT-LIB tasks. 3 seeds for primary; 1 seed for ablations.\n\n**Table 1 (Exec-only dynamics, 3 seeds)**: Prove rate 0.18→0.33 over 500 steps, ISR/PR ratio degrades 0.92→0.35.\n\n**Table 2 (Exec+STA dynamics, 3 seeds)**: Prove rate 0.16→0.34 (matches exec-only), ISR/PR stabilizes at 0.65. 7% wall-clock overhead.\n\n**Table 3 (Held-out)**: Exec-only PR=0.24, ISR/PR=0.33, Hack%=45. Exec+STA PR=0.26, ISR/PR=0.85, Hack%=18. Base model ISR/PR=0.53 (higher than trained exec-only).\n\n**Table 4 (O(1) vs O(N))**: O(1) tautology-only check has discriminability 0.08 vs O(N)'s 0.72. ISR/PR=0.15 (worse than exec-only's 0.35).\n\n**Table 5 (Weight ablation, 1 seed)**: 6 conditions (3 α/β ratios × {fixed, curriculum}). Curriculum Pareto-dominates fixed. Best: curriculum 0.5/0.5 (PR=0.30, ISR/PR=0.65, product=0.195).\n\n**Table 6 (Prolog, 1 seed)**: Exec-only ISR/PR=0.32, Exec+STA ISR/PR=0.58.\n\n**Table 7 (Three-logic summary)**: FOL (0.35→0.65), Prolog (0.32→0.58), SMT-LIB (0.38→0.52). Discriminability: 0.72, 0.45, 0.35.\n\n**Table 8 (Multi-logic training)**: Modest cross-logic benefits. Prolog +0.01 PR from multi-logic. ISR/PR slightly lower under multi-logic.\n\n**Table 9 (Zero-shot cross-logic transfer)**: Positive but small transfer (mean Δ=0.045 over base). STA-trained +0.01 additional Δ vs exec-only.\n\n**Table 10 (SFT baseline)**: SFT PR=0.20, ISR/PR=0.65. GRPO exec+STA PR=0.30, ISR/PR=0.63. SFT gets structure from human demos but lower accuracy.\n\n**Table 11 (4B vs 8B scaling)**: Both scales show ~0.28 ISR/PR gap between exec-only and exec+STA. 8B: exec-only 0.40, exec+STA 0.68.\n\n**Table 12 (Hacking taxonomy, 200 outputs, κ=0.78)**: Exec-only total hack rate 45%, exec+STA 18%, base 25%, SFT 12%. Tautological premises: 18%→3% under STA.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nAll 12 planned experiments are covered in the paper:\n1. FOL Exec-Only 500-Step Baseline (plan_001) - 3 seeds ✓\n2. FOL Exec+STA 500-Step Primary (plan_002) - 3 seeds ✓\n3. FOL Held-Out Evaluation (plan_003) ✓\n4. O(1) vs O(N) Ablation (plan_004) - 1 seed ✓\n5. Weight Ablation (plan_005) - 1 seed each ✓\n6. Prolog Single-Logic (plan_006) - 1 seed ✓\n7. Multi-Logic GRPO (plan_007) - 1 seed ✓\n8. Cross-Logic Transfer (plan_008) ✓\n9. SFT Baseline (plan_009) ✓\n10. SMT-LIB Single-Logic (plan_010) - 1 seed ✓\n11. Qwen3-8B Scaling (plan_011) - 1 seed ✓\n12. Hacking Characterization (plan_012) ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodology Soundness** (strengths and weaknesses of the STA formulation, ISR metric, combined reward, 5-step protocol, RIP mapping)\n2. **Experimental Rigor** (statistical robustness, seed counts, ablation coverage, potential confounds, metric validity)\n3. **Reproducibility** (sufficient detail to replicate?)\n4. **Specific concerns or questions** for the authors\n5. **Score** (1-6) with justification\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. Identify both strengths and weaknesses. Consider: Are the claims well-supported? Are there missing baselines or ablations? Are effect sizes meaningful? Are there potential confounds? Is the evaluation metric (ISR) well-validated?\n\nOutput your review in structured markdown format with clear section headers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39311,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39311},"output_tokens":15,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db36cc0b-3691-41e1-9901-01ea32ffa94b","uuid":"16c49b07-95c7-42e7-b261-f9af926b593d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"afa6dcf289673c424","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JsSqVCrsrp5pzzVYEXXtj6","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Solver Trace Ablation: Dense Structural Rewards for Formal Reasoning via Mutation Testing\"\n\nThe paper proposes Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a reward design methodology grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing. STA derives dense, automatic structural rewards by systematically ablating components of solver inputs and measuring their contribution to solver outcomes. The core metric is the Intensional Structure Ratio (ISR) = (1/n) Σ 𝟙[S(f_{-i}) ≠ S(f)], measuring the fraction of structurally necessary components.\n\nThe combined reward is r(f) = α·r_exec(f) + β·r_exec(f)·ISR(f), with a sigmoid curriculum for β.\n\nA 5-step derivation protocol (pilot → collect shortcuts → classify → map RIP → design checks) instantiates STA for any solver-language pair.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Setup**: Qwen3-4B (primary), Qwen3-8B (scaling). GRPO with K=8, 500 steps. FOLIO dataset for FOL; equivalent Prolog/SMT-LIB tasks. 3 seeds for primary; 1 seed for ablations.\n\n**Table 1 (Exec-only dynamics, 3 seeds)**: Prove rate 0.18→0.33 over 500 steps, ISR/PR ratio degrades 0.92→0.35.\n\n**Table 2 (Exec+STA dynamics, 3 seeds)**: Prove rate 0.16→0.34 (matches exec-only), ISR/PR stabilizes at 0.65. 7% wall-clock overhead.\n\n**Table 3 (Held-out)**: Exec-only PR=0.24, ISR/PR=0.33, Hack%=45. Exec+STA PR=0.26, ISR/PR=0.85, Hack%=18. Base model ISR/PR=0.53 (higher than trained exec-only).\n\n**Table 4 (O(1) vs O(N))**: O(1) tautology-only check has discriminability 0.08 vs O(N)'s 0.72. ISR/PR=0.15 (worse than exec-only's 0.35).\n\n**Table 5 (Weight ablation, 1 seed)**: 6 conditions (3 α/β ratios × {fixed, curriculum}). Curriculum Pareto-dominates fixed. Best: curriculum 0.5/0.5 (PR=0.30, ISR/PR=0.65, product=0.195).\n\n**Table 6 (Prolog, 1 seed)**: Exec-only ISR/PR=0.32, Exec+STA ISR/PR=0.58.\n\n**Table 7 (Three-logic summary)**: FOL (0.35→0.65), Prolog (0.32→0.58), SMT-LIB (0.38→0.52). Discriminability: 0.72, 0.45, 0.35.\n\n**Table 8 (Multi-logic training)**: Modest cross-logic benefits. Prolog +0.01 PR from multi-logic. ISR/PR slightly lower under multi-logic.\n\n**Table 9 (Zero-shot cross-logic transfer)**: Positive but small transfer (mean Δ=0.045 over base). STA-trained +0.01 additional Δ vs exec-only.\n\n**Table 10 (SFT baseline)**: SFT PR=0.20, ISR/PR=0.65. GRPO exec+STA PR=0.30, ISR/PR=0.63. SFT gets structure from human demos but lower accuracy.\n\n**Table 11 (4B vs 8B scaling)**: Both scales show ~0.28 ISR/PR gap between exec-only and exec+STA. 8B: exec-only 0.40, exec+STA 0.68.\n\n**Table 12 (Hacking taxonomy, 200 outputs, κ=0.78)**: Exec-only total hack rate 45%, exec+STA 18%, base 25%, SFT 12%. Tautological premises: 18%→3% under STA.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nAll 12 planned experiments are covered in the paper:\n1. FOL Exec-Only 500-Step Baseline (plan_001) - 3 seeds ✓\n2. FOL Exec+STA 500-Step Primary (plan_002) - 3 seeds ✓\n3. FOL Held-Out Evaluation (plan_003) ✓\n4. O(1) vs O(N) Ablation (plan_004) - 1 seed ✓\n5. Weight Ablation (plan_005) - 1 seed each ✓\n6. Prolog Single-Logic (plan_006) - 1 seed ✓\n7. Multi-Logic GRPO (plan_007) - 1 seed ✓\n8. Cross-Logic Transfer (plan_008) ✓\n9. SFT Baseline (plan_009) ✓\n10. SMT-LIB Single-Logic (plan_010) - 1 seed ✓\n11. Qwen3-8B Scaling (plan_011) - 1 seed ✓\n12. Hacking Characterization (plan_012) ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodology Soundness** (strengths and weaknesses of the STA formulation, ISR metric, combined reward, 5-step protocol, RIP mapping)\n2. **Experimental Rigor** (statistical robustness, seed counts, ablation coverage, potential confounds, metric validity)\n3. **Reproducibility** (sufficient detail to replicate?)\n4. **Specific concerns or questions** for the authors\n5. **Score** (1-6) with justification\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. Identify both strengths and weaknesses. Consider: Are the claims well-supported? Are there missing baselines or ablations? Are effect sizes meaningful? Are there potential confounds? Is the evaluation metric (ISR) well-validated?\n\nOutput your review in structured markdown format with clear section headers.","uuid":"eb826f5c-bfc9-4e3a-8635-287a19952e9d","session_id":"db36cc0b-3691-41e1-9901-01ea32ffa94b"}
