{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BJe8tSUDUbSvNtVbbwu5eF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Eu2S1KTZPxF7d5pYErFkqS","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty**, **Related Work**, and **Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Solver Trace Ablation: Dense Structural Rewards for Formal Reasoning via Mutation Testing\"\n\nThe paper proposes Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a reward design methodology grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing. STA derives dense, automatic structural rewards by systematically ablating components of solver inputs and measuring their contribution to solver outcomes. The core metric is the Intensional Structure Ratio (ISR) = (1/n) Σ 𝟙[S(f_{-i}) ≠ S(f)], measuring the fraction of structurally necessary components.\n\nCombined reward: r(f) = α·r_exec(f) + β·r_exec(f)·ISR(f), with sigmoid curriculum for β.\n\n5-step derivation protocol: pilot → collect shortcuts → classify → map RIP → design checks.\n\n## Key Claims\n1. **General derivation protocol** for structural rewards via RIP framework, demonstrated for FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3\n2. **STA fills the dense+automatic cell** in ORM-PRM taxonomy (ORM = sparse+automatic, PRM = dense+manual)\n3. **Pareto-improving** over exec-only GRPO: matching prove rate, better structural integrity\n4. **O(1) negative result**: coarse approximations fail, motivating full O(N) ablation\n5. **Scale-invariant** benefits from 4B to 8B\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper\n- **RL for Formal Reasoning**: Polu & Sutskever 2020 (theorem proving), Le et al. 2022 (CodeRL), Mellgren et al. 2025 (Prolog structure-aware RL), EVOM 2026 (cross-solver GRPO)\n- **Structural Rewards**: PRMs (Lightman et al. 2023), ExecVerify/EGCA (code execution traces), Mellgren et al. hand-crafted Prolog penalties\n- **Mutation Testing**: Jia & Harman 2011 (RIP framework)\n\nOnly 6 references total in the paper.\n\n## Experimental Evidence Summary\n- FOL: exec-only ISR/PR degrades 0.92→0.35; exec+STA stabilizes at 0.65 (3 seeds, 500 steps)\n- Held-out: ISR/PR 0.33 (exec-only) vs 0.85 (exec+STA), hack rate 45%→18%\n- Prolog, SMT-LIB: same qualitative pattern with diminishing magnitude\n- SFT baseline: good structure (0.65 ISR/PR) but lower accuracy (0.20 vs 0.30)\n- 8B scaling: ISR/PR gap preserved (~0.28 at both scales)\n- Weight ablation: curriculum Pareto-dominates fixed; best is curriculum 0.5/0.5\n- O(1) check: discriminability 0.08, ISR/PR 0.15 (worse than exec-only)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea? Is the RIP→STA adaptation genuinely new, or straightforward? Is ISR a significant conceptual contribution? How does it compare to existing structural reward approaches?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning fair and accurate? Only 6 references seems very sparse for a venue paper.\n3. **Conceptual Clarity**: Is the connection between mutation testing and reward design well-motivated? Is the ORM-PRM taxonomy positioning accurate?\n4. **Broader Impact and Limitations**: Are limitations adequately discussed? What are the broader implications?\n5. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n6. **Specific concerns or questions** for the authors\n7. **Score** (1-6) with justification\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. Consider: Is the novelty sufficient for a top venue? Is the theoretical grounding genuine or window-dressing? Is the related work coverage adequate? Are there closely related works that are not cited?\n\nOutput your review in structured markdown format with clear section headers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39311,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39311},"output_tokens":15,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db36cc0b-3691-41e1-9901-01ea32ffa94b","uuid":"8905b2d0-5979-4186-8e07-ddbdf61c6483"}
